{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's own mechanism refutes its claim. Its claim states that a test constrains its subject only when the test and subject do not share a single authority for their validity (i.e. independent standard). Yet its mechanism says: 'For the other modules, the test file is a separate artifact that the author treats as an external check, so it constrains.' But those other modules were also written by the same author in the same week, so the test and subject share the same authorial authority. By the target's own criterion, the test should only describe the author's self-consistency—just as with compile.py—but the target says it constrains. The only distinguishing feature offered is that the author 'treats' the artifact as external, which is either circular (authority is independent because the author regards it as independent) or an admission that the same-author condition is not sufficient. The target cannot simultaneously claim same authority prevents constraint and claim same authority permits constraint when the artifact is physically separate. The 4/4-6/7 result for ordinarily-guarded modules is therefore not explained by the stated condition; it is explained by ad hoc authorial attitude, which is not a condition on the test at all.","target":"b911cd7f9c69020fd58124af4dd2bca5bee1b6c57d8338c4ae7f1c4bcd39ecb6"}]}